#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + ( e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad\n\nQ(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3,\n$$\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  P}{\\partial  y}} = - 3 {y}^{2} , \\hspace{2em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial  x}} = 3 {x}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\underset{C}{∮} ( {e}^{- {x}^{2}} - {y}^{3} ) \\mathrm{d} x + ( {e}^{- {y}^{2}} + {x}^{3} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\mathrm{d} y & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( \\frac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial  x} - \\frac{\\partial  P}{\\partial  y} ) \\text{d} A \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( 3 {x}^{2} - ( - 3 {y}^{2} ) ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{d} A \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } 3 ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{d} A .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\underset{D}{\\iint } 3 ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} )  \\text{d} A &  = \\underset{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}{\\iint } 3 ( {r}^{2} {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {r}^{2} {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  )  r  \\text{d} r \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{2} 3 {r}^{3} ( {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  )  \\text{d} r  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{2} 3 {r}^{3}  \\text{d} r  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {[ \\frac{3 {r}^{4}}{4} ]}_{0}^{2}  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } 12  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = 12 [ \\theta  {]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  &  = 12 \\pi \\end{array}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731036 :question-generator/id #uuid "d656df16-d9af-57aa-99f8-1931f28b483a" :question-generator/script "generators/5e/5e893c016efa46160322f8f10c139e482d7f63ece3fb0b13dda974b37f97c88d/17592186176023.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f2c80d-9cac-5756-9929-d92d4f8f3e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416075} {:answer/id #uuid "168b8872-373b-550c-a253-e7487bbea803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416076} {:answer/id #uuid "40901d5c-3eac-5579-8462-7e9d79976125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416080} {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} {:answer/id #uuid "7691659c-2eed-5772-8397-8d23f26bb55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} :answer-field/id #uuid "064abd0a-e4c9-5ad6-9b82-ef5bf4dea054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416103}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac600244-dbff-596b-9fbb-094ec0801e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109119" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(4-x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21230e40-c778-5976-8975-77b9141fa55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539545} {:answer/id #uuid "82f5e165-d4c5-5f87-9f4c-f1a35e90641d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539695} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7d42e8-02bc-575f-bdc0-3241bc078dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539711} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b99d4-e511-50b9-9301-30b784d9db25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539819} {:answer/id #uuid "eae586ab-e6cd-5a7e-8c86-9db144eadecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56b99d4-e511-50b9-9301-30b784d9db25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539819} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f67038-add1-5bf7-87fe-fead30bf61e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "552ffe88-dfcd-57ba-97f8-f7bf0a708429" :question/math-academy-id "q-125717" :question/problem "![](images/66/66ab1a3e2e7244a9b3e5fc9bda814736194c28dd9438fbb97086e99be4633261.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/70/709d108b2b992d1714c7c0b3bef26f52e965c2467f38d05103cd45f4f9bc821b.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{y}^{2}-(-4{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06945f99-44ac-5640-b54f-bce0077123f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259935} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1ac15a-5785-5358-9f30-8bef0b1aff07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261522} {:answer/id #uuid "d37903c6-09d2-5a3f-bf75-c74f7c5fbdec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261735} {:answer/id #uuid "e512847b-77b3-5cd2-87d1-d23710510d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261890} {:answer/id #uuid "ff79ed24-25be-5e20-bded-431d31365687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff79ed24-25be-5e20-bded-431d31365687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262115} :answer-field/id #uuid "889bd836-2a14-565c-95a2-9c72a89ea2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262362}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a75ae5f-913c-5dd5-a1ba-18a248f691d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126733" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨2-{x}^{2}y,1+x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(1+x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d0/d081f4dc19484f0c483c5518295c7c3b7195bb096404fb7ff6e27d8bda1cccc1.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=1+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(1+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{625}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b2453-2340-5b1a-b035-13af6216e390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260350} {:answer/id #uuid "678d8b1a-b4fb-5ecd-9b33-15222113a168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260779} {:answer/id #uuid "a144f162-db84-521f-aac9-d31fa7f5c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261277} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d7ee8d-a24f-5376-9f95-9be5fc03201d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261467} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e469d8-39d1-5c40-aeb7-1753437104ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e469d8-39d1-5c40-aeb7-1753437104ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d81e6d-01cf-501c-b61e-4c6039a60547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "388e817a-4cb8-54d8-b3b7-fede3e086b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126736" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}x-2{x}^{2}y,\\text{ }\\hspace{-0.167em}\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}x-2{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/90/902790a00a049bbfc759403125ad5f19a8c2528a46b59c721ac43fb137cd5d8e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261c0683-b5fd-5378-9f79-2f86d8986e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260208} {:answer/id #uuid "662bd605-3ab9-5488-a002-c8fa16319af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260763} {:answer/id #uuid "8b57ae42-c1de-5f2a-b039-be8db7894670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261090} {:answer/id #uuid "97f70e2c-3eb9-57dc-b585-0a203ce8b1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261193} {:answer/id #uuid "f5992f2b-a16d-5638-b86c-c0c08b116db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662bd605-3ab9-5488-a002-c8fa16319af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260763} :answer-field/id #uuid "de116324-372b-5aa0-acac-810021212cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262508}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec2130ce-f120-59d6-bb98-ec9c31641d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-126747" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e8aa9acdca87acf153b2e7ad3f5e51d5708f53b81562e682e16098721d752a.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/92/922ae907661e21ea5510dc0d02f151afc4f4423ad11a572ede6849c623397baa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={2}^{y}+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 4,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}-{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-32[\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}0] \\\\  & =-16\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0683defb-b18a-5104-ba2b-2d172de9437a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259934} {:answer/id #uuid "1a07b2c9-5a32-5442-aa95-42c397e429db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260098} {:answer/id #uuid "2551ce74-532c-5f28-af57-3ffd6ddde697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260200} {:answer/id #uuid "71f89f57-5c33-5f0e-9d29-6a394cf2c9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871} {:answer/id #uuid "fd24ebdc-0496-5e63-96aa-2f262b5a9c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2551ce74-532c-5f28-af57-3ffd6ddde697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260200} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e227f31-00b9-570c-bcee-be2f54e3ed07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262405}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c830bb6a-6822-5681-99a1-ffb4d98dc8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126755" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c8/c8ebdae5c3fb2c2ca7b318f32a357589d73bbe0d6471877cbf8babd813edd395.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\,dx+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}3({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =3(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a22b31-2af0-5b06-9a83-6c218c2bccec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260038} {:answer/id #uuid "268d0b01-2071-5ce9-af67-d8d07bc7d498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260213} {:answer/id #uuid "27fcc03b-e7ec-5cd6-8c17-d9ed415fbdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260226} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8ff48a-760c-518f-a7a3-eb1811f9959f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261115} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aa5623-cd60-5cc3-8f6a-687f5cc6e34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "268d0b01-2071-5ce9-af67-d8d07bc7d498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260213} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c9c6b7-94d1-5f0d-8201-97dc531b22fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0c6144e-7780-5bef-857b-0b92844c7ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126756" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d675e8d06f2037531a2eec3a7e48374bb0d2e5cb59adfee0cbee5ce3a23add67.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/96/96998e4a17d20459622486970518a677309b23fd936c993d8c97dcf0d65a9a29.png)\n\n \n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{81}{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e4cdf-338e-59c7-bd21-7afe84d888b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} {:answer/id #uuid "36abd901-c8ba-5d38-a5c9-52c30378dadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260363} {:answer/id #uuid "9d666cad-5bfd-565d-8739-349a89a1addf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bca1ec-b5b5-53a5-8432-35d6656bef12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261314} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5fdf8c-c1cf-53bf-9f78-dc3ffff862be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150e4cdf-338e-59c7-bd21-7afe84d888b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcd46d2-644b-54bb-992b-feba4db98108" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5041cff1-c6ce-564b-b2c4-39a44d615f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-126758" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcdc0f59fa284c2b1f1621e7de057ef6f0e084d2eca6732fbc5eae3f74ede113.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/62/6273034b38059d3d49b2a29c91052c290151b4a29073f1c925172dd1c4c2bf72.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{2}-(-3{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}3({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}60\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =180[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =180\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =90\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed58d08-ecaa-599c-9195-c29b35269e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260015} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa6614-b6f1-5782-8504-d218d250802f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157} {:answer/id #uuid "94458430-8e1f-5e26-b6c8-6071eb5169db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261166} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d8f66-99a1-572a-81e7-43c27f0e448c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261511} {:answer/id #uuid "dcac3080-ad1d-58a2-89d2-fd5a233d7141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93aa6614-b6f1-5782-8504-d218d250802f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157} :answer-field/id #uuid "2887dfa3-45a9-5407-8ca4-04a9ffa5e4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262198}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "546a427c-f103-5c76-afb8-9ff327ad2cbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-126759" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}⟩\n$$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dx+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-(-{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf2dceb-7c5d-5d9b-9403-414a88730866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416074} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5d7b5b-5c88-5245-85a2-eb913d758a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{729\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416083} {:answer/id #uuid "96c867f3-7baa-5a33-8eba-e465e7463140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416090} {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} {:answer/id #uuid "abee63b5-df6a-5f86-838c-404f4f8edb05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} :answer-field/id #uuid "07315ac9-2ead-596d-85c1-f7d066447650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "654eb25e-4dd2-5095-910d-a25787a1e455" :question/math-academy-id "q-126753" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x-{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x-{y}^{3},\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x-{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}3({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\hspace{-0.167em}\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458839} {:answer/id #uuid "8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458874} {:answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} {:answer/id #uuid "a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458925} {:answer/id #uuid "e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459095}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126735" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f3/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458702} {:answer/id #uuid "64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458803} {:answer/id #uuid "8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458877} {:answer/id #uuid "b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458946} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459196}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126737" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046b8a59-298b-5353-a076-d3cb1f14b26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562766} {:answer/id #uuid "2d382635-c61f-5270-95b7-d95a5118de4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562771} {:answer/id #uuid "525e5813-b0e0-5b2f-b02a-34e14302f53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562779} {:answer/id #uuid "53db2eee-8b16-55f9-9f2e-6764503fae01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562780} {:answer/id #uuid "d2264d89-bb92-5918-ae83-5b25039c7085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "525e5813-b0e0-5b2f-b02a-34e14302f53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562779} :answer-field/id #uuid "7478ebc9-6334-518d-87d3-a180009d37c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a3db414-03cf-5eab-8959-e39595655eb0" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d2/d2ee5fe463ad086cce741c62866c38c7ea331b343bdb0ae82ab64a0f8e730a1e.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-4x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+4xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 4 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(4 y^{2}-(- 4 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 4(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(4-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}4\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=16[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=16\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e068a41-14e7-5690-b28f-edda3fe55d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562781} {:answer/id #uuid "7991b15a-1528-5b1a-98c0-e9d13c902dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562787} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c88df5-7474-5d10-8f70-02edf6058bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562796} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c5551f-db27-51b2-a4e4-8eeab465ff43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562802} {:answer/id #uuid "ec208b84-8a21-5c5d-ae53-9fe4aaddaacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec208b84-8a21-5c5d-ae53-9fe4aaddaacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562813} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa8050d-43f2-5b47-aa1b-e9ca68027231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562784}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36d37f77-94a1-5750-955e-00c0cfbaff25" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/eed043488a6cd3949da95907125dcdfa2f946feefff60772495e48e9610c4bc1.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{1}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2960d72a-0a54-5b88-bee3-242a1e3b4640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562770} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1bf688-5389-548e-93fa-5d1ff024bc7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35 \\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562777} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a982a0-af6f-52ff-a451-b9eb2139b210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562798} {:answer/id #uuid "be47f539-04eb-5d2b-a660-466609a747e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562803} {:answer/id #uuid "eadab8b3-b89c-5b00-b770-5f624dc9857c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7a982a0-af6f-52ff-a451-b9eb2139b210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562798} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6ee701-817f-5ced-bd0b-110fb570a0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65f7dca3-dec5-557b-aa20-0b4ae8f4395f" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3ba969ce97031cecfc6c60ce908a082b268c11a474e6a588b9719ef2ce39e0d2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^{-y^2}+5xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 5 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(5 y^{2}-(- 5 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 5(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{1}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{15}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc866a-de1a-5704-a1c1-c913f66680cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562767} {:answer/id #uuid "8e790f2e-095d-54af-90a9-fd9fe370115e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562792} {:answer/id #uuid "a81ed047-e215-52ab-be23-78353a509fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562799} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1bbe4-2e9d-5071-8a5f-7c69fc53bbc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562806} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4f42c-f65a-56dd-976e-09a6674be37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4f42c-f65a-56dd-976e-09a6674be37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562810} :answer-field/id #uuid "32e7c13a-cd78-5a43-8f0d-eab158b59557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562772}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f78c19f-91c3-570f-9d07-d78ab8ff655f" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/50/50aabd9d34ec0f889ca832e181e271e3e5918f936708684a125fadcdb1cdb71f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=64[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=64\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b9caf01-baab-5870-b7d6-820bbcfe7d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562775} {:answer/id #uuid "5f46e0f2-1fa8-54b3-9ddb-9b5bf7e64d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562782} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d2472-d403-511f-8eb1-f6085c89b973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562789} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6bcc-7b62-526c-870d-099dc45a4c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562791} {:answer/id #uuid "efb5810f-7897-5409-a195-83c50f8b7d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f46e0f2-1fa8-54b3-9ddb-9b5bf7e64d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2685de-a28f-510f-b37e-732196580698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac95db02-2900-5a0e-a6b1-4da40e53b041" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a9a9d6-02ad-5f5b-ba6e-05886838a7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562774} {:answer/id #uuid "5195a77d-03e2-5c78-823d-910cf0023ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562778} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d24f9d-8887-5080-b78e-d5e5f67348f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562797} {:answer/id #uuid "d751fde0-0034-5a42-bdad-6904561a00a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562809} {:answer/id #uuid "eba33b1d-8e12-5cdc-ae2a-8c3766a6a8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "486 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d751fde0-0034-5a42-bdad-6904561a00a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562809} :answer-field/id #uuid "419fba28-082c-5d89-8e47-9823b5bee043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfddd2c6-0361-5659-b47a-55d51d0fa39a" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c046036-74c5-5283-bafb-a0b9acc21ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562785} {:answer/id #uuid "873ce346-c394-5d0c-8dac-2be89d8dab57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562788} {:answer/id #uuid "91d8d73c-4c69-5274-9c4e-28c4d051d857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562794} {:answer/id #uuid "a97d9f64-2f3b-55cf-85d3-b94f3836a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1536 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562800} {:answer/id #uuid "bf108011-dd23-5024-bd01-352a6c5ed1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873ce346-c394-5d0c-8dac-2be89d8dab57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562788} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d781142-e26e-5675-b4b3-e5ca51c4c2e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6252a58-b771-5462-82ca-89e1f399e18c" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f0/f070b659b344abe432eb2218cddc02947cecca2e7dafb89a6bf3a49dc4ba06af.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=192[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=192\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=384 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}]}
